drills.explanation:
One query for the orders, one for the distinct customers, then a lookup by id:

```js
const orders = await db.listOrders();
const ids = [...new Set(orders.map((o) => o.customerId))];
const byId = new Map();
if (ids.length > 0) {
  for (const c of await db.customersByIds(ids)) byId.set(c.id, c);
}
return orders.map((o) => ({ id: o.id, total: o.total, customerName: byId.get(o.customerId)?.name ?? null }));
```

The tests catch two plausible-wrong versions. Matching the batch result by position (`rows[ids.indexOf(id)]`) assumes the database returns rows in the requested order and returns one row per id; it does neither, so names land on the wrong orders. Passing every order's customer id straight to the batch asks for the same customer 200 times and sends an empty list when there are no orders, which Postgres rejects as a syntax error.

