drills.explanation:
Collect the keys of one tick in a `Map` (which also de-duplicates them), dispatch it in a microtask, then settle each key by position:

```js
if (pending === null) {
  const batch = new Map();
  pending = batch;
  queueMicrotask(() => { pending = null; dispatch(batch); });
}
```

Inside `dispatch`, check `values.length === keys.length` before resolving anything, and reject every waiter if `batchFn` fails. A `Map` keeps insertion order, so `[...batch.keys()]` is the first-requested order. Each key keeps one promise, so every caller of that key gets the same value.

The tests catch two plausible-wrong versions. A queue that batches correctly but pushes every call keeps duplicate keys (`[1, 2, 1]`) and trusts the result length, so a short result silently resolves loads with the wrong rows or `undefined`. Calling the batch function once per `load` is just the N+1 again.

