drills.explanation:
Subscribe, await `fn` inside `try`, unsubscribe in `finally`, then compare:

```js
const queries = [];
const off = db.onQuery((sql) => queries.push(sql));
let result;
try {
  result = await fn();
} finally {
  off();
}
if (queries.length > max) {
  const list = queries.map((q, i) => `  ${i + 1}. ${q}`).join('\n');
  throw new Error(`query budget exceeded: ${queries.length} queries, budget ${max}\n${list}`);
}
return { result, queries };
```

The tests catch two plausible-wrong versions. Using `>=` fails a handler that uses its budget exactly, and calling `off()` only on the success path leaves a listener behind when `fn` throws, so every later test in the file counts queries that are not its own. A message that only says "3 queries, budget 2" is correct but not answerable: the test failure should show which query repeated.
